      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing the Split: Testing Spatial Repetition and Camera Position in Syndromes and a Century",
        "central_argument": "A filmmaker can test whether framing, camera position, and spatial repetition distinguish the film's two halves by treating the film as a controlled formal experiment. The record confirms a two-part structure, and attributed criticism reports that the halves present similar stories with different details and contrasting atmospheres—warm and intimate versus cold and detached. Rather than relying on remembered scenes, the filmmaker should log how the camera frames recurring spatial motifs (rooms, corridors, landscapes, architectural details) and whether the same or similar spaces are revisited with different camera positions, lens choices, or staging patterns. The central hypothesis is that the film's formal repetition of space, combined with subtle shifts in camera placement and framing, creates a structural rhyme that makes the tonal shift between halves legible without explicit narration. This approach turns the film's bipartite design into a testable formal relation: if the same spatial elements recur but are framed differently, the filmmaker can infer that camera position and framing are doing significant work in differentiating the halves. The study must remain conditional because no clip evidence is supplied; every claim about specific shots is a viewing hypothesis to be verified against the film.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Camera position and spatial continuity",
          "Framing and offscreen space",
          "Spatial repetition and staging patterns",
          "Editing and spatial construction",
          "Camera movement and spatial transfer",
          "Screen direction and spatial orientation"
        ],
