      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing Distrust: Testing Uncertainty in The Thing",
        "central_argument": "The Thing’s horror of group mistrust can be studied as a formal problem: how do framing, eyelines, and editing organise uncertainty without resolving it? Rather than asserting that the film uses specific techniques to create paranoia, this study proposes a series of viewing hypotheses that treat every formal observation as conditional. Drawing on continuity editing, vector-line analysis, and montage theory, the sections below develop a cumulative argument: the film’s editing may systematically destabilise spatial and temporal coherence, its eyelines may withhold or misdirect point-of-view, and its shot scales may compress or isolate bodies in ways that make trust visually unverifiable. Each section advances a distinct formal relation, from temporal order to graphic matching to rhythmic acceleration, and each ends with a precise logging task. Because no clip evidence is supplied, all claims remain hypotheses to be tested against the film itself.",
        "section_lenses": [
